I don't know Slash'em at all; is it actually a descendant of NetHack or a
re-write?

If it's derived from NetHack, it probably uses a similar recovery command
(called "recover", of course).

If you're on a Unix, "man recover" should help.

The game files should be in what the installation calls "NETHACKDIR", often
/usr/lib/nethackdir (try "locate nethackdir" or try "which nethack" and look in
that file, which is usually a shell script, for NETHACKDIR), and be called
"[letter]lock.[number]".  The "-d directory" referred to on the man page would
be "-d [wherever NETHACKDIR is]", the "base[number]" referred to in the man
page would be "[letter]lock".

So, for example, it'd be something like "recover -d /usr/lib/nethackdir alock".

This will merge the checkpoint files into a saved game file, which it _usually_
puts back in place correctly though on some setups it doesn't set the
permissions correctly or compress the file, so it may not load without you
manually fixing that.  Look in "NETHACKDIR/save" for the new file if it won't
load correctly and verify that it's the same permissions and compression status
as the other saved games that may be there.

Then again, you may not be on Unix and Slash'em may not be like NetHack in this
regard.  ;-)

